          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A 60-year-old man reports blood appearing only at the end of his urinary stream,</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A 60-year-old man reports blood appearing only at the end of his urinary stream, with the rest of the stream clear. Which part of the urinary tract is the most likely source of bleeding?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Urethra or prostate</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Initial haematuria — blood at the start of the stream that then clears — points to the urethra or prostate. Blood at the end indicates a different site.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Kidney or ureter</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Renal or ureteric bleeding produces total haematuria, with blood mixed throughout the stream, because it enters the bladder continuously and is voided from start to finish.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Bladder neck or trigone</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Terminal haematuria localises the source to the bladder neck or trigone. These structures are squeezed during the final phase of voiding, releasing blood at the end of the stream.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Glomerulus</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Glomerular bleeding is identified by dysmorphic red cells and red-cell casts on microscopy, not by stream timing. It typically presents as non-visible or diffusely discoloured urine.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Review the stream-timing classification: initial haematuria (urethra/prostate), terminal haematuria (bladder neck/trigone), and total haematuria (bladder or upper tract)."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct. Stream timing is a bedside clue: initial = urethra/prostate, terminal = bladder neck/trigone, total = bladder body or upper tract."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
                "enableRetry": true,
                "enableSolutionsButton": true,
