          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A 55-year-old man presents with a single episode of painless visible haematuria.</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A 55-year-old man presents with a single episode of painless visible haematuria. Urinalysis shows isomorphic red blood cells with no casts. A renal ultrasound is reported as normal. What is the most appropriate next step?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Reassure the patient and advise return only if symptoms recur</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Painless visible haematuria demands a complete work-up before any reassurance. Bladder cancer bleeds intermittently — a single normal investigation does not close the case.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Refer to nephrology for glomerular evaluation</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Nephrology referral is indicated when microscopy shows dysmorphic red cells, red-cell casts, or significant proteinuria — features of glomerular disease. Isomorphic cells keep the case on the surgical pathway.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Proceed with CT urogram and flexible cystoscopy</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Isomorphic red cells indicate a surgical source. CT urogram evaluates the upper tract while cystoscopy visualises the bladder — each detects lesions the other misses, and both are mandatory before the work-up is complete.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Start empirical antibiotics for a presumed urinary tract infection</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>There is no clinical indication of infection — no dysuria, no fever. Painless visible haematuria requires malignancy exclusion, not empirical antibiotics.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Remember the non-negotiable rule: unexplained painless visible haematuria requires both CT urogram (upper tract) and flexible cystoscopy (bladder) before the work-up is closed."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct. CT urogram and cystoscopy are complementary and both mandatory — ultrasound alone cannot exclude urothelial malignancy."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
                "enableRetry": true,
