          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>In bladder cancer, which single finding on biopsy most critically determines whe</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>In bladder cancer, which single finding on biopsy most critically determines whether the bladder can be preserved or must be removed?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Histological grade of the tumour (low-grade versus high-grade)</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Grade influences prognosis and the choice of adjuvant intravesical therapy, but it is the depth of invasion — specifically whether the detrusor muscle is breached — that determines whether the bladder can be saved.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Invasion of the detrusor muscle</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The muscle-invasion divide is the pivotal decision point. Non-muscle-invasive tumours (Ta, T1, CIS) are managed with TURBT and intravesical therapy, preserving the bladder. Once the detrusor is invaded (T2 or above), radical cystectomy with urinary diversion is required.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Presence of squamous differentiation</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Squamous differentiation is relevant in schistosomiasis-endemic regions where chronic infection drives squamous cell carcinoma, but it does not determine the preserve-versus-remove decision — depth of invasion does.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Number and size of tumours seen on cystoscopy</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Multifocality and tumour size affect recurrence risk and surveillance intensity, but the decision to preserve or remove the bladder hinges on whether the muscle layer has been invaded.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The critical divide is whether the tumour has invaded the detrusor muscle. Non-muscle-invasive disease (Ta, T1, CIS) allows bladder preservation; muscle-invasive disease (≥T2) requires radical cystectomy."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct. The muscle-invasion divide is the most important question in bladder cancer management — it separates bladder-sparing treatment from radical cystectomy."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
