          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A 52-year-old woman has a 3.5 cm renal mass confined to the kidney on contrast-e</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A 52-year-old woman has a 3.5 cm renal mass confined to the kidney on contrast-enhanced CT, with no evidence of renal vein thrombus or metastases. What is the most appropriate surgical management?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Radical nephrectomy with excision of Gerota's fascia</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Radical nephrectomy is reserved for larger or locally advanced renal tumours. For a T1a lesion (≤4 cm, confined to the kidney), partial nephrectomy provides equivalent oncological control while preserving functioning renal tissue.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Nephron-sparing partial nephrectomy</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>A T1a renal mass (≤4 cm, confined to the kidney) is optimally managed with partial nephrectomy. Preserving renal parenchyma protects long-term kidney function without compromising cancer outcomes.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Transurethral resection of the tumour</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Transurethral resection (TURBT) is the procedure for bladder tumours, not renal masses. Renal tumours require open or laparoscopic excision — either partial or radical nephrectomy.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>First-line tyrosine kinase inhibitor therapy</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Tyrosine kinase inhibitors and immune checkpoint therapy are reserved for metastatic renal cell carcinoma. Localised disease is managed with curative surgery — the tumour is resectable and there are no metastases.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "For a small (≤4 cm) renal mass confined to the kidney with no metastases, partial nephrectomy is preferred over radical nephrectomy to preserve renal function. RCC resists radiotherapy and chemotherapy — surgery is curative."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct. Nephron-sparing partial nephrectomy is the standard of care for T1a renal cell carcinoma, balancing oncological safety with renal function preservation."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
